10:11:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Blood is never actually blue. It is always some shade of red. - **Oxygen-rich blood** (usually in arteries) is **bright red**. - **Oxygen-poor blood** (usually in veins) is **darker, deep red/maroon**. Veins can *look* blue or green through skin because of how light passes through and scatters in your skin and tissues. Skin absorbs more red light and reflects/scatters more of the shorter blue wavelengths back to your eyes, making the vein appear bluish—even though the blood inside is dark red. Blood does not turn red simply from hitting the air. When blood becomes oxygenated—normally in the lungs—hemoglobin changes to a brighter red form. A sample of dark venous blood may look somewhat brighter after exposure to air as it picks up oxygen, but it was red all along.
